1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,999
Pull a loaded cart along a road with a rope that slopes upward.

2
00:00:03,999 --> 00:00:09,233
Only part of your pull moves the cart forward: the green part, along the road.

3
00:00:09,233 --> 00:00:10,447
The rest just lifts.

4
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:16,033
Make the rope steeper and the useful part shrinks; pull straight up and it is zero.

5
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:19,236
The work done is F times d times cos theta.

6
00:00:19,236 --> 00:00:23,663
That way of multiplying two arrows to get a number is the dot product.

7
00:00:23,663 --> 00:00:28,355
There is a second way, which gives an arrow, and this unit is about both.

8
00:00:33,833 --> 00:00:38,435
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 9: Product of Vectors.

9
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:42,098
Five parts.

10
00:00:42,098 --> 00:00:46,277
The scalar, or dot, product and the angle between vectors.

11
00:00:46,277 --> 00:00:49,337
Projections and the rules of the dot product.

12
00:00:49,337 --> 00:00:52,605
The dot product in geometry and trigonometry.

13
00:00:52,605 --> 00:00:55,691
The vector, or cross, product.

14
00:00:55,691 --> 00:01:00,424
And what the cross product measures: areas, moments and the sine rules.

15
00:01:04,767 --> 00:01:14,026
The scalar, or dot, product of a and b is the length of a, times the length of b, times the cosine of the angle between them.

16
00:01:14,026 --> 00:01:16,930
The answer is a number, not a vector.

17
00:01:16,930 --> 00:01:18,962
Swing b round and watch its sign.

18
00:01:19,167 --> 00:01:21,356
Acute angle: positive.

19
00:01:21,356 --> 00:01:27,255
At exactly ninety degrees the cosine is zero, and so is the dot product.

20
00:01:27,255 --> 00:01:29,130
Obtuse: negative.

21
00:01:29,130 --> 00:01:33,273
So a zero dot product is the test for perpendicular vectors.

22
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,015
In components it is even easier.

23
00:01:36,015 --> 00:01:42,278
Each unit vector dotted with itself is one, and with a different one is zero.

24
00:01:42,278 --> 00:01:51,481
So the dot product is a one b one plus a two b two plus a three b three, and the cosine of the angle is the dot product over the two lengths.

25
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:53,656
The standard question.

26
00:01:53,933 --> 00:01:56,769
Multiply matching components and add: four.

27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,180
The lengths: three and seven.

28
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:04,866
Cos theta is four over twenty one, so theta is seventy nine degrees.

29
00:02:06,867 --> 00:02:08,036
Test each corner.

30
00:02:08,267 --> 00:02:13,557
For the angle at B, both vectors must start at B: B A and B C.

31
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:17,894
Their dot product is zero, so the angle at B is a right angle.

32
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:24,758
A careful point: for the angle at B, both arrows must start at B.

33
00:02:24,758 --> 00:02:29,769
Using A B instead of B A gives you one eighty minus the angle.

34
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:36,964
Shine a light straight down onto b.

35
00:02:36,964 --> 00:02:41,274
The shadow that a casts on b is the projection of a on b.

36
00:02:41,274 --> 00:02:48,905
Its length is a cos theta, which is the dot product divided by the length of b, the vector you project onto.

37
00:02:48,905 --> 00:02:53,010
Multiply by the unit vector along b and you get the vector projection.

38
00:02:54,233 --> 00:02:56,669
One dot product serves three answers.

39
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:01,010
Dot product twenty one; lengths root fourteen and seven.

40
00:03:01,233 --> 00:03:06,456
Divide by the length of b, the one you project onto: three.

41
00:03:06,456 --> 00:03:10,781
For the vector, multiply b by twenty one over forty nine.

42
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,824
And the angle: thirty six point seven degrees.

43
00:03:15,833 --> 00:03:16,863
The rules.

44
00:03:16,863 --> 00:03:18,204
Order does not matter.

45
00:03:18,204 --> 00:03:20,483
It distributes over addition.

46
00:03:20,483 --> 00:03:24,454
A vector dotted with itself is its length squared.

47
00:03:24,454 --> 00:03:30,340
So the length of a plus or minus b, squared, expands just like algebra.

48
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:33,569
And a zero dot product means perpendicular.

49
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:35,856
No components needed.

50
00:03:36,067 --> 00:03:43,113
Square the sum and expand: twenty five equals nine plus sixteen plus twice the dot product.

51
00:03:43,113 --> 00:03:46,628
So the dot product is zero: perpendicular.

52
00:03:46,628 --> 00:03:48,861
A three-four-five right angle.

53
00:03:49,067 --> 00:03:51,304
The difference has the same length, five.

54
00:03:53,333 --> 00:03:53,957
Back to work.

55
00:03:54,567 --> 00:03:56,574
Displacement: head minus tail.

56
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,386
Work is the dot product: twenty joules.

57
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:03,384
And the force is twenty two degrees off the direction of motion.

58
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:06,573
And one trap.

59
00:04:06,573 --> 00:04:13,175
You cannot divide by a vector: a dot b equals a dot c does not mean b equals c.

60
00:04:13,175 --> 00:04:16,073
It only says a is perpendicular to b minus c.

61
00:04:20,867 --> 00:04:24,553
The dot product proves geometry with almost no effort.

62
00:04:24,553 --> 00:04:30,999
In a parallelogram on a and b, the diagonals are a plus b and a minus b.

63
00:04:30,999 --> 00:04:36,116
Their dot product expands to length a squared minus length b squared.

64
00:04:36,116 --> 00:04:43,957
That is zero exactly when the sides are equal: the diagonals of a parallelogram are perpendicular only in a rhombus.

65
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:49,374
And the cosine law of unit six falls out in one line.

66
00:04:49,374 --> 00:04:59,409
The third side is a minus b. Dot it with itself: a squared plus b squared minus two a dot b, which is minus two a b cos C.

67
00:05:00,633 --> 00:05:02,457
Trigonometry from vectors.

68
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:06,796
Take unit vectors at forty five and thirty degrees.

69
00:05:06,796 --> 00:05:10,317
The angle between them is fifteen, and both have length one.

70
00:05:10,533 --> 00:05:15,860
So their dot product is cos fifteen: root six plus root two, over four.

71
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:18,613
The same trick proves cos A minus B in general.

72
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:28,616
The second product, the vector or cross product, gives a vector.

73
00:05:28,616 --> 00:05:36,376
Its length is a b sin theta, which is exactly the area of the parallelogram on a and b.

74
00:05:36,376 --> 00:05:45,538
Its direction is perpendicular to both, chosen by the right hand rule: curl your fingers from a to b, and your thumb points along a cross b.

75
00:05:45,767 --> 00:05:50,834
Swap the order and the thumb flips: b cross a is minus a cross b.

76
00:05:50,834 --> 00:05:56,546
And parallel vectors span no area, so their cross product is the zero vector.

77
00:05:57,767 --> 00:06:04,644
To compute it, write a determinant: the unit vectors on top, a in the second row, b in the third.

78
00:06:04,644 --> 00:06:13,508
Take a equal to two i plus j plus three k, and b equal to three i plus five j minus two k.

79
00:06:13,733 --> 00:06:22,485
For the i part, cover the i column and cross-multiply what is left: one times minus two, minus three times five.

80
00:06:22,485 --> 00:06:23,756
Minus seventeen.

81
00:06:23,967 --> 00:06:29,216
For j, cover the j column, cross-multiply, and change the sign.

82
00:06:29,216 --> 00:06:32,588
That middle minus sign is where most marks are lost.

83
00:06:32,588 --> 00:06:33,430
Plus thirteen.

84
00:06:33,633 --> 00:06:38,869
For k, cover the k column: two times five minus one times three.

85
00:06:38,869 --> 00:06:39,292
Seven.

86
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:51,364
So a cross b is minus seventeen i plus thirteen j plus seven k. Check: dot it with a and you get minus thirty four plus thirteen plus twenty one, zero.

87
00:06:51,364 --> 00:06:52,873
Perpendicular, as it must be.

88
00:06:54,100 --> 00:06:55,197
The classic use.

89
00:06:55,400 --> 00:07:02,124
Anything perpendicular to both is parallel to a cross b: minus six, six, minus three.

90
00:07:02,333 --> 00:07:03,827
Its length is nine.

91
00:07:04,033 --> 00:07:11,644
Divide, and remember both directions: plus or minus a third of minus two, two, minus one.

92
00:07:13,667 --> 00:07:15,014
Linking the two products.

93
00:07:15,233 --> 00:07:25,966
Since cos squared plus sin squared is one, the dot product squared plus the cross product's length squared equals the product of the lengths squared: Lagrange's identity.

94
00:07:26,167 --> 00:07:34,673
So the dot product is plus or minus sixty: the data fixes sin theta, but not whether theta is acute or obtuse.

95
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:38,472
Keep the two products apart.

96
00:07:38,472 --> 00:07:41,832
The dot product is a number and does not care about order.

97
00:07:41,832 --> 00:07:45,589
The cross product is a vector, and order flips its sign.

98
00:07:51,367 --> 00:07:56,401
The length of the cross product is the area of the parallelogram on the two vectors.

99
00:07:56,633 --> 00:08:03,200
A triangle is half of that parallelogram: half the cross product of two sides from the same vertex.

100
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:08,928
If you only know the diagonals of a parallelogram, its area is half their cross product.

101
00:08:09,133 --> 00:08:16,736
And three points are on one line exactly when the triangle they form has no area: the cross product is zero.

102
00:08:17,967 --> 00:08:19,329
A triangle in space.

103
00:08:19,533 --> 00:08:21,539
Two sides from the same vertex A.

104
00:08:21,767 --> 00:08:24,884
Cross them: minus four, two, minus one.

105
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:29,477
Half its length: root twenty one over two, about two point two nine.

106
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:32,469
Zero area.

107
00:08:32,733 --> 00:08:36,829
A C is exactly twice A B: the same direction.

108
00:08:37,033 --> 00:08:41,463
So the cross product is the zero vector, and the three points lie on one line.

109
00:08:43,467 --> 00:08:46,646
Physics uses the cross product for turning.

110
00:08:46,646 --> 00:08:50,513
A force F acts at a point with position r from a pivot O.

111
00:08:50,513 --> 00:08:53,547
Its moment, or torque, is r cross F.

112
00:08:53,547 --> 00:08:59,325
Its size is the force times the perpendicular distance d from O to the line of the force.

113
00:09:00,533 --> 00:09:02,551
A moment in three dimensions.

114
00:09:02,767 --> 00:09:04,383
r is three, two, two.

115
00:09:04,383 --> 00:09:07,816
Cross with F: minus eight, eight, four.

116
00:09:08,033 --> 00:09:12,353
Its size is twelve newton metres; the force is three newtons.

117
00:09:12,567 --> 00:09:15,700
So the line of action passes four metres from O.

118
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:19,995
Trigonometry again, with the other product.

119
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:23,301
Take unit vectors at minus B and at A.

120
00:09:23,301 --> 00:09:28,420
The angle between them is A plus B, so their cross product has length sin A plus B.

121
00:09:28,633 --> 00:09:33,812
In components, that cross product is sin A cos B plus cos A sin B.

122
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:39,347
With A forty five and B thirty: root six plus root two over four.

123
00:09:39,347 --> 00:09:41,590
The same as cos fifteen, as it must be.

124
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:45,570
Pause and think.

125
00:09:52,867 --> 00:09:56,879
a is perpendicular to b: cos theta is zero.

126
00:09:56,879 --> 00:09:59,444
A zero cross product would mean parallel.

127
00:10:01,433 --> 00:10:01,868
One more.

128
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:12,405
Half the cross product of the diagonals.

129
00:10:12,405 --> 00:10:14,658
The full cross product is for the sides.

130
00:10:16,833 --> 00:10:17,941
Quick recap.

131
00:10:17,941 --> 00:10:26,613
The dot product is a number, a b cos theta, zero for perpendicular vectors, and gives projections and work.

132
00:10:26,613 --> 00:10:34,061
The cross product is a vector perpendicular to both, whose length is an area, and whose order matters.

133
00:10:34,061 --> 00:10:37,954
It gives triangle areas, collinearity and moments.

134
00:10:37,954 --> 00:10:42,397
And together they prove the compound angle formulas.

135
00:10:46,567 --> 00:10:48,313
That was product of vectors.

136
00:10:48,313 --> 00:10:54,082
Next, correlation and regression: measuring how two quantities move together.

137
00:10:54,082 --> 00:10:54,658
See you there.

